{"name":"Duck’s Egg Blue","hex":"#ccdfe8","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"duck","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To quickly lower (the head or body), often in order to prevent it from being struck by something.","To lower (something) into water; to thrust or plunge under liquid and suddenly withdraw."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A cave passage containing water with low, or no, airspace."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/duck"},{"term":"egg","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An approximately spherical or ellipsoidal body produced by birds, insects, reptiles, and other animals, often housing an embryo within a membrane or shell during its development.\n\n  The (usually) non-developing, edible egg (sense 1.1) of a domestic fowl such as a duck, goose, or, especially, chicken;  the contents of such an egg or eggs used as food.","An approximately spherical or ellipsoidal body produced by birds, insects, reptiles, and other animals, often housing an embryo within a membrane or shell during its development.\n\n  The (usually) non-developing, edible egg (sense 1.1) of a domestic fowl such as a duck, goose, or, especially, chicken;  the contents of such an egg or eggs used as food."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To throw (especially rotten) eggs (noun sense 1.1.1) at (someone or something).","To throw (especially rotten) eggs (noun sense 1.1.1) at (someone or something)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/egg"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Egg","blocks":[{"text":"An egg is an organic vessel grown by an animal to carry a possibly fertilized egg cell – a zygote. Within the vessel, an embryo is incubated until it has developed enough to survive on its own, at which point the animal hatches. Reproductive structures similar to the egg in other kingdoms are termed \"spores\", or in spermatophytes \"seeds\", or in gametophytes \"egg cells\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Adolphe_Millot_oeufs-fixed.jpg/330px-Adolphe_Millot_oeufs-fixed.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Egg"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"},{"title":"Duck","blocks":[{"text":"Duck is the common name for numerous species of waterfowl in the family Anatidae. Ducks are generally smaller and shorter-necked than swans and geese, which are members of the same family. Divided among several subfamilies, they are a form taxon; they do not represent a monophyletic group, since swans and geese are not considered ducks. Ducks are mostly aquatic birds, and may be found in both fresh water and sea water."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Bucephala-albeola-010.jpg/330px-Bucephala-albeola-010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Duck"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Adolphe_Millot_oeufs-fixed.jpg/500px-Adolphe_Millot_oeufs-fixed.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Adolphe_Millot_oeufs-fixed.jpg/1280px-Adolphe_Millot_oeufs-fixed.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Adolphe_Millot_oeufs-fixed.jpg/500px-Adolphe_Millot_oeufs-fixed.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Eggs of various birds, a turtle, various cartilaginous fish, a cuttlefish and various butterflies and moths. (Click on image for key)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOeufs002b.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Salmoneggskils.jpg/500px-Salmoneggskils.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Salmoneggskils.jpg/1280px-Salmoneggskils.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Salmoneggskils.jpg/500px-Salmoneggskils.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASalmoneggskils.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Salmonlarvakils.jpg/500px-Salmonlarvakils.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Salmonlarvakils.jpg/500px-Salmonlarvakils.jpg?utm_source=en.wikip